{"title":"Estimating pass@k","sub":"From n = 20 samples per problem, for three problems of different difficulty.","kind":"plot","interval":3000,"plot":{"xmin":1,"xmax":20,"ymin":0,"ymax":1.04,"ticks":[1,5,10,15],"xlabel":"k","h":230,"yticks":[[0.5,"0.5"],[1,"1"]]},"legend":[["a","c = 1 of 20 passed"],["b","c = 4"],["good","c = 10"],["muted","dashed: plug-in estimate"]],"frames":[{"caption":"Generate n = 20 samples per problem and run the unit tests. If c of them pass, the unbiased estimate of pass@k (the chance that at least one of k fresh samples passes) is 1 − C(n − c, k) / C(n, k). A hard problem with c = 1 (blue): pass@1 = 0.05, but pass@20 = 1, since one of the 20 did pass.","curves":[{"tok":"a","pts":[[1,0.050000000000000044],[2,0.09999999999999998],[3,0.15000000000000002],[4,0.19999999999999996],[5,0.25],[6,0.30000000000000004],[7,0.35],[8,0.4],[9,0.44999999999999996],[10,0.5],[11,0.55],[12,0.6],[13,0.65],[14,0.7],[15,0.75],[16,0.8],[17,0.85],[18,0.9],[19,0.95],[20,1.0]],"w":2.5}],"points":[],"stats":[["n",20],["c = 1: pass@1 / pass@10","0.05 / 0.50"]]},{"caption":"c = 4 (orange) and c = 10 (green): pass@k rises steeply with k. Allowing several attempts and checking them with tests turns a modest pass@1 into a high pass@10, which is why papers must say which k they report.","curves":[{"tok":"a","pts":[[1,0.050000000000000044],[2,0.09999999999999998],[3,0.15000000000000002],[4,0.19999999999999996],[5,0.25],[6,0.30000000000000004],[7,0.35],[8,0.4],[9,0.44999999999999996],[10,0.5],[11,0.55],[12,0.6],[13,0.65],[14,0.7],[15,0.75],[16,0.8],[17,0.85],[18,0.9],[19,0.95],[20,1.0]],"w":2.5},{"tok":"b","pts":[[1,0.19999999999999996],[2,0.368421052631579],[3,0.5087719298245614],[4,0.6243550051599587],[5,0.7182662538699691],[6,0.7933952528379773],[7,0.8524251805985552],[8,0.8978328173374613],[9,0.9318885448916409],[10,0.956656346749226],[11,0.9739938080495356],[12,0.9855521155830753],[13,0.9927760577915377],[14,0.9969040247678018],[15,0.9989680082559339],[16,0.9997936016511868],[17,1.0],[18,1.0],[19,1.0],[20,1.0]],"w":2.5},{"tok":"good","pts":[[1,0.5],[2,0.7631578947368421],[3,0.8947368421052632],[4,0.956656346749226],[5,0.9837461300309598],[6,0.9945820433436533],[7,0.9984520123839009],[8,0.9996427720885925],[9,0.9999404620147654],[10,0.9999945874558878],[11,1.0],[12,1.0],[13,1.0],[14,1.0],[15,1.0],[16,1.0],[17,1.0],[18,1.0],[19,1.0],[20,1.0]],"w":2.5}],"points":[],"stats":[["n",20],["c = 1: pass@1 / pass@10","0.05 / 0.50"],["c = 4: pass@1 / pass@10","0.20 / 0.96"],["c = 10: pass@1 / pass@10","0.50 / 1.00"]]},{"caption":"Dashed: the tempting plug-in formula 1 − (1 − c/n)^k, which treats c/n as the true success rate. Averaged over many evaluations it comes out too low (1 − (1 − p)^k is concave in p, so plugging in a noisy estimate drags it down), and here it is 0.06 below the unbiased value for c = 4 at k = 10. That bias is why Chen et al. (2021) introduced the unbiased estimator when they released HumanEval.","curves":[{"tok":"a","pts":[[1,0.050000000000000044],[2,0.09999999999999998],[3,0.15000000000000002],[4,0.19999999999999996],[5,0.25],[6,0.30000000000000004],[7,0.35],[8,0.4],[9,0.44999999999999996],[10,0.5],[11,0.55],[12,0.6],[13,0.65],[14,0.7],[15,0.75],[16,0.8],[17,0.85],[18,0.9],[19,0.95],[20,1.0]],"w":2.5},{"tok":"a","pts":[[1,0.050000000000000044],[2,0.09750000000000003],[3,0.1426250000000001],[4,0.18549375000000012],[5,0.22621906250000023],[6,0.2649081093750002],[7,0.3016627039062503],[8,0.33657956871093775],[9,0.3697505902753909],[10,0.4012630607616213],[11,0.43119990772354033],[12,0.45963991233736334],[13,0.4866579167204952],[14,0.5123250208844705],[15,0.536708769840247],[16,0.5598733313482347],[17,0.5818796647808229],[18,0.6027856815417818],[19,0.6226463974646927],[20,0.6415140775914581]],"w":1.5,"dash":true},{"tok":"b","pts":[[1,0.19999999999999996],[2,0.368421052631579],[3,0.5087719298245614],[4,0.6243550051599587],[5,0.7182662538699691],[6,0.7933952528379773],[7,0.8524251805985552],[8,0.8978328173374613],[9,0.93188